Modifications made by gmlxo76:

v0.2.0 (2026-05-11):
- Added /codex-image:style-gen command for generating new images whose visual
  style matches an attached reference image. The reference is attached via
  `codex exec --image` and role-labeled as a style reference (not an edit
  target), per the Codex imagegen skill's role classification.
- New STYLE_GEN_INSTRUCTION_PREFIX in scripts/codex-image.mjs.
- New skills/style-gen/SKILL.md.

v0.3.0 (2026-05-11):
- Added /codex-image:asset-pipeline command — high-order orchestration of
  style-gen for batch asset production. The Claude Code agent plans an asset
  list from a project context, confirms with the user, saves a manifest, and
  runs style-gen sequentially per item with a sample-first gate above 10
  items.
- New `parse-args` dispatcher subcommand (arg validator used by the
  asset-pipeline skill).
- New skills/asset-pipeline/SKILL.md.
- Updated renderStatusReport usage lines and dispatcher usage() to list the
  new commands.

v0.3.1 (2026-05-11):
- Sprite sheet generation rules baked into skills/asset-pipeline/SKILL.md
  (Sprite Sheet Conventions section): uniform cell size, anchored sprites,
  contained VFX, consistent style across frames, standard frame counts,
  1/4/8-direction conventions. Mandatory verification step after each sheet.
- skills/style-gen/SKILL.md gets a shorter mirror of the rules for direct
  style-gen sheet invocations.
- Motivated by a real test where the first generated sprite sheet had
  drifting anchors and effect overflow across rows.

v0.3.2 (2026-05-11):
- Asset-pipeline planning rules in SKILL.md now require inferring beyond
  the reference UNIVERSALLY (every domain, not just games — SaaS dashboards,
  e-commerce, mobile apps, banking, social, restaurant menus all get the
  same treatment). The agent must ask, for every visible element, "what
  other screens/states/assets must exist around this one?" and plan
  whatever it answers "obviously also exists." Includes multi-domain
  checklist of conventional unseen-but-required assets to add.
- Explicitly warns against under-spec'ing UI (a single in-product screen
  typically implies 10-15 UI sheets — never group into one "ui-misc"
  bucket).
- Inferred items flagged `inferred: true` in manifest so the user can trim.
- Motivated by five consecutive under-spec passes during the asset-pipeline
  test where the agent missed entire asset categories until the user
  called them out individually, then explicitly asked the rule generalize
  beyond games to "anything — whatever the reference depicts."

